'''Finques Superiors, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Finques Superiors}}; "Superior Estates") is a Balboan real estate development company specialising in luxury high-rise residential towers. The company is the premier developer of high-end residential properties in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Sant Bart]], the country's two largest metropolitan areas, and its developments are among the most prestigious addresses in the Balboan real estate market. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker FNS.

Founded in 1985 by [[August Vidal i Puig]], a Sant Cristòfor real estate developer, Finques Superiors targeted the upper end of the Balboan residential market — a segment that was underserved during the post-reunification period, when the major developers were focused on mid-market apartments and suburban housing. The company's developments are characterised by high-rise towers with luxury amenities, premium locations, and high price points, and its brand is associated with exclusivity and architectural quality.

The company's portfolio is concentrated in two markets: the [[Sant Cristòfor]] metropolitan area, where its towers are concentrated in the waterfront and financial districts, and [[Sant Bart]], where its developments are located in the city's highland centre and surrounding affluent districts.

== History ==

=== Founding and early years (1985–1995) ===

[[August Vidal i Puig]] established Finques Superiors in 1985, during the late stages of the [[Balboa real-estate bubble]]. Vidal, who had inherited a portfolio of rental properties in the Districte Financer from his father, recognised that the growing wealth of the Sant Cristòfor financial sector was creating demand for luxury residential properties that the existing developers were not supplying. The major developers — [[Construccions de Sant Cristòfor|CSC]], [[Cases Justes]], and others — were focused on mid-market apartments and suburban housing, and the luxury segment was served only by small-scale developers building individual high-end buildings.

The company's first development was the '''Torre Vidal''' (1988), a 28-storey luxury residential tower in the Districte Financer, overlooking the bay. The tower was the first purpose-built luxury high-rise in Sant Cristòfor, and its units — which featured large floor plans, private balconies, and building amenities including a pool, fitness centre, and concierge service — sold at prices that were approximately three times the per-square-metre average for the Sant Cristòfor market.

The Torre Vidal established the template for Finques Superiors' subsequent developments: high-rise towers in premium locations, with large units, luxury amenities, and premium pricing. The company's early developments were concentrated in the Districte Financer and the waterfront areas of Sant Cristòfor, where the financial sector's growth was producing a class of affluent professionals who were willing to pay premium prices for luxury housing.

The [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] and the [[1994 Balboa spot recession]] tested the company's model. The luxury market was hit hard by the crisis, as the speculative bubble that had inflated real estate prices collapsed. However, Finques Superiors had been more conservative than many developers: the company had sold units in its towers before construction, reducing its inventory risk, and its developments in the Districte Financer retained value better than the speculative properties in the outer suburbs. The company weathered the crisis without major losses, though several planned developments were deferred.

=== Expansion into Sant Bart (1995–2010) ===

In the late 1990s, Finques Superiors expanded into the [[Sant Bart]] market. Sant Bart, the interior's largest city and the administrative centre of [[Jala Province|Jala]], had a growing affluent class — interior business owners, cooperative executives, and professionals — who were seeking luxury housing but had limited options. The city's highland setting, with its cooler climate and mountain views, was attractive to the interior elite, and Finques Superiors identified the market as underserved.

The company's first Sant Bart development was the '''Torre de la Serra''' (1999), a 22-storey tower in the city centre with views of the Yalabirga range. The tower was the first luxury high-rise in Sant Bart, and it established the company's brand in the interior market. Subsequent Sant Bart developments were concentrated in the city's affluent districts, with towers ranging from 18 to 30 storeys.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 2000, with the Vidal family retaining a 35 per cent stake. The listing raised capital that was used to fund the expansion of the Sant Bart portfolio and to develop larger, more ambitious projects in the Sant Cristòfor market.

The 2000s saw the company develop increasingly ambitious towers, including the '''Torre de la Badia''' (2006), a 42-storey tower on the Sant Cristòfor waterfront that was the tallest residential building in Balboa at the time of its completion. The Torre de la Badia became the company's flagship development and a symbol of the Sant Cristòfor luxury market.

=== Modern era (2010–present) ===

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 initially reduced demand for luxury properties, but the subsequent recovery produced a surge in demand for high-end residential properties, as affluent buyers sought larger homes with premium amenities and private outdoor space. Finques Superiors' developments, with their large units and building amenities, were well-positioned for the shift, and the company's sales volumes and prices increased significantly in the 2021–2024 period.

By 2026, Finques Superiors had completed 24 luxury towers — 16 in Sant Cristòfor and 8 in Sant Bart — comprising approximately 3,200 residential units. The company had four towers under construction and employed approximately 2,200 people. The Vidal family retained a 30 per cent stake.

The company has been the subject of public criticism regarding the affordability of its developments and the impact of luxury development on the Sant Cristòfor housing market. Critics have argued that the concentration of luxury towers in the Districte Financer and the waterfront has contributed to the gentrification of these areas and has reduced the supply of mid-market housing. The company has responded that its developments serve a distinct market segment that would otherwise be served by foreign developers, and that its projects contribute to the modernisation of the urban landscape.

== Operations ==

Finques Superiors operates as a single-division developer focused exclusively on the luxury high-rise market:

* '''Luxury high-rise development''': The company designs, builds, and sells luxury residential towers in premium locations in Sant Cristòfor and Sant Bart. The company's towers range from 18 to 42 storeys and typically include 80 to 200 units, with floor plans ranging from 120 to 400 square metres. Building amenities include pools, fitness centres, concierge services, private parking, and landscaped communal areas. The company's developments are concentrated in the Districte Financer and waterfront areas of Sant Cristòfor and in the affluent districts of central Sant Bart.

The company's headquarters are at 350 Carrer Frederic, in the Districte Financer of Sant Cristòfor, and it maintains a regional office in Sant Bart.

